[ ] Deliver the agent surface ~ $CLI-first orientation and capture, then a thin rmcp wrapper, then a dogfood verdict$ ^2026-09-14T17:00 #01a0a25d-6197-79f0-a91c-113e3a4b3665
    >[=] Scaffold clearhead mcp over the CLI library $NEEDS DECISION: which crate boundary for the MCP surface -- move commands + sparql + dataset into clearhead-cli's lib.rs (large, whole-CLI refactor), or host the curated surface in the binary (contradicts the recorded lib.rs mechanism but preserves its intent)? Full analysis, costs and recommendation below.rmcp stdio server, read-only first: orient, show, query_named, plus resources for charters, named queries and the ontology. DECIDED 2026-09-18: not the whole commands module. orient::build already returns pure data (Orient struct) separately from orient::run's printing - that split is exactly the shape MCP needs. Apply the same build()/run() split to show and query_named's command functions, then expose only the build()-side functions (plus their return types) as a new pub mod mcp_support in clearhead-cli's lib.rs - a narrow, curated surface, not commands::* wholesale. clearhead mcp is a SUBCOMMAND of the existing clearhead binary (matching okf's own 'okf mcp \\\\\\\[bundle]' pattern), not a new crate - add Verb::Mcp to argparser.rs, dispatch to commands::mcp::run, built on rmcp. Gate rmcp and this subcommand behind a new optional 'mcp' feature (matching how 'sparql' is gated) so the minimal --no-default-features build stays free of it - verify with the same oxigraph-leak-style dependency check the wasm gate uses. capture/transact wait on 01a05b5d and 01a0b2c2 (both must land first); this action is unblocked today and covers only orient/show/query_named as read-only tools plus the three resources.STOPPED 2026-09-18 (overnight clearhead-core run, task 7/7 — the stretch item). No code was written; nothing was started, so nothing is half-migrated. The decision recorded here (2026-09-18, platform 3f2f12e) cannot be executed as written, and the gap is exactly the one the superseded version of this same note flagged: "commands::* is currently `mod commands;` declared privately in clearhead-cli's main.rs, not re-exported from lib.rs ... 'clearhead mcp is a thin wrapper over the same library functions' isn't true yet — decide how much of commands becomes pub ... before writing the wrapper, not while writing it." That paragraph was replaced by the DECIDED text, which names the mechanism ("a new pub mod mcp_support in clearhead-cli's lib.rs") but not how the lib reaches the code, and the dependency facts below show the mechanism is out of reach without a further crate-boundary call.WHAT I VERIFIED (facts, not readings):1. `mod commands;` is declared in crates/clearhead-cli/src/main.rs (bin-private). lib.rs has no `commands` module and never sees `CommandContext`.2. `orient::build` — the one function the decision calls "already pure data" and the obvious first tool — takes `&CommandContext` and calls `crate::sparql::index::nodes_for(ctx, "unscheduled")` for its `unscheduled` section. `mod sparql` is likewise declared in main.rs, behind the `sparql` feature. So the *narrow* surface is not narrow in dependency terms: the lib cannot host `orient::build` without also hosting the bin-private `sparql` module (and therefore `dataset`, which the query layer assembles the workspace RDF through), and with them most of the binary.3. `show` has no build side at all today: `show_action` resolves an action and prints `display::render_action_detail`; `show_charter`/`show_plan` print tables. So "apply the same build()/run() split" is not extraction of an existing value — it is first *designing* the JSON shape an MCP client would receive for an action, a charter, and a plan (the CLI has no structured show output to split).4. Everything else the scaffold needs does exist and is verified available: rmcp 3.4.0 (features server+macros+transport-io), tokio, and schemars all resolve and compile OFFLINE from the local cargo cache (probe: a scratch crate depending on rmcp 3.4.0 built clean in 8.75s); the gate is versioned at clearhead-core/.githooks/pre-push and already has the oxigraph-leak check to copy for rmcp; `orient::build -> Orient` is Serialize and already prints JSON when stdout is not a terminal.THE UNDECIDED CALL — how the curated surface coexists with a bin-private module tree, in order of cost:(a) Relocate `commands` + `sparql` + `dataset` + `stdout` (or equivalents) into clearhead-cli's lib.rs, make main.rs a thin consumer, and add the narrow `pub mod mcp_support` on top. Honors the recorded mechanism literally, but it is a crate-boundary refactor of every verb's `crate::` paths (argparser/display/environment_reader/telemetry already live in the lib; stdout/dataset/sparql do not), gated by a feature that would move with it. Large, touches the whole CLI, and is not something to start unsupervised at the end of a run.(b) Keep the MCP server in the bin (commands/mcp.rs calling commands::orient::build and new sibling build fns) and let it be the curated surface. This preserves the decision's *intent* — a narrow surface, never commands::* wholesale — while contradicting its stated location (lib.rs). One line of the decision would need revising.(c) Split by dependency: move only what carries no query-engine dependency (the native sections of orient, and a designed `show` build) into lib.rs, and either defer `query_named` until the engine moves or accept a degraded orient in MCP. Cheapest, but MCP's orient would silently differ from the CLI's when `sparql` is on, which is worse than either (a) or (b).RECOMMENDATION: (a) or (b), decided deliberately, then this scaffold is mechanical — rmcp is offline-available, the feature-gate pattern (feature + `#\\\[cfg(not(feature))]` runtime bail + a dependency check in .githooks/pre-push) is established by `sparql`, and the three resources (charters, named queries, ontology) are reads over data that already exists. Also worth folding in with (3) above: decide the MCP `show` payload shape now, since an agent-facing schema is a decision, not an extraction.Also note for whoever picks this up: with 01a05b5d and 01a0b2c2 both landed tonight (2026-09-18), the sibling `Add capture and transact MCP tools` action is no longer blocked on its two predecessors — it now waits only on this scaffold and on the same crate-boundary call.$ ^2026-09-14T17:00 <01a0b5a4-ec3f-76ad-a63a-ca655a83364c #01a0a25d-86be-7d20-bead-8c865af74d6f
    >[ ] Add capture and transact MCP tools $Same delivery path as the CLI; capture records provenance when promoting an agent finding into a charter — concretely, an optional promoted_from citing an agent-workspace claim id, written into the action note or charter log line, so the promotion is auditable later (the reverse link, a claim's external_reference pointing at a charter, already exists per the charter README). transact stays scoped to update/complete/cancel; deliberately not extended to add/delete for MCP convenience — creation and destruction deserve their own friction, not a shared batch surface. Both predecessors give agents a real conflict signal instead of a stringified error before any of this writes concurrently.$ ^2026-09-14T17:00 <01a05b5d-62be-73c0-8e70-154d78bd57a3 <01a0b2c2-f791-733e-b323-6b16f3e7d24a #01a0a25d-86cc-7182-b6b9-5e8c656d5e38
    >[ ] Dogfood with two agent clients and record the verdict $measure whether findings get captured mid-task without reminders, including ambient capture from an agent todo loop; log the verdict in the support charter$ ^2026-09-14T17:00 #01a0a25d-86db-73b2-9b9a-02ee3b21c79d
[ ] Decide how an action is assigned to an agent or person $NEEDS DECISION: what standards-based identity does an assignee carry, and what does the DSL syntax for assigning look like? The user wants standards-based contact information (the calendar and contact standards) but has not found a syntax that is not ugly in the DSL. Not tonight. Why it matters, found 2026-09-18 planning the second overnight run: nothing records who did or owns a piece of work, so a per-agent queue is impossible. The overnight commits are authored as the human and the model was known only from a transcript; agent-workspace state is scoped by worktree, not by actor; and the unscheduled queue cannot be filtered to one agent. Do not use context tags as a stand-in: a context says where or when an action can be done, not who does it, and using one for an assignee would put the wrong meaning in the ledger and leave a migration. Interim provenance, no DSL change: a commit trailer Agent colon model slash run id, plus the run branch and worktree (added to the successor overnight runbook). Once assignment exists, a saved named query in .clearhead/queries (a copy of unscheduled filtered to one assignee) is the per-agent queue, and the runbooks stop listing action ids.$ !4 ^2026-09-18T22:23 #01a0b81e-b609-7552-a75e-66bdfbcb3637
[ ] Record an attempt on an action as a fact =action-attempt-fact $Direction from the agent-sandbox charter: ClearHead is aware of everyone, nobody is aware of ClearHead, so the link between an action and the run that worked it lives in ClearHead's data model, pointing outward. An attempt is a temporal fact about an action, like a completion: when, by whom or what, the outcome of completed, blocked or unfinished, and the commits it produced. Commits are the durable join key; run directories are disposable. Design it in the spec and ontology first, following plans-are-facts; the sandbox driver then records attempts through the CLI.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-4b0c-7b60-a98c-5dbb7ac0cf29
